### What version of Codex CLI is running?

codex-cli 0.163.0-alpha.11 (npm package `@openai/codex@0.163.0-alpha.11-win32-x64`). The code involved is unchanged on `main` at 941b32b.

### What subscription do you have?

Not relevant (the failure happens before any tool runs)

### Which model were you using?

Not relevant

### What platform is your computer?

Microsoft Windows NT 10.0.26200.0 x64 (Windows 11 25H2), Windows PowerShell 5.1

### What terminal emulator and version are you using (if applicable)?

Non-interactive over OpenSSH

### Codex doctor report

Not included. The repro below runs without a Codex session.

### What issue are you seeing?

If the path to `codex-code-mode-host.exe` is 260 characters or longer, Codex can't start the code-mode host, so every tool call fails. The host exists and `is_file()` returns true, but `CreateProcessW` reports that the path doesn't exist:

```text
ERROR codex_core::tools::router: error=failed to spawn code-mode host C:\Users\<user>\AppData\Local\mise\installs\npm-openai-codex\0.163.0-alpha.11~aube~8fd2c8af89d0c98c\node_modules\.mise\@openai+codex-win32-x64@0.163.0-alpha.11-win32-x64\node_modules\@openai\codex-win32-x64\vendor\x86_64-pc-windows-msvc\bin\codex-code-mode-host.exe: The system cannot find the path specified. (os error 3)
```

I hit this with Codex installed through mise's npm backend, which nests the platform package deeply. With the real user name, `codex.exe` is 249 characters long, so the shell can still start it. `codex-code-mode-host.exe` sits in the same `bin` folder and is 264 characters long. The agent's only output was: "the command runner cannot find `codex-code-mode-host.exe`". A byte-identical copy of the same `vendor\x86_64-pc-windows-msvc` folder at a short path works.

Any install location deep enough to push the host past 259 characters triggers this, for example a long user name, a nested project-local `node_modules`, or a pnpm-style virtual store. `LongPathsEnabled` doesn't help: it was already 1 on this machine. 8.3 short names are disabled on the volume, so they can't be used as a workaround either.

### Root cause

1. [`InstallContext::code_mode_host_program`](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/941b32bf57e520f506cda9741de615f64f53fbe6/codex-rs/install-context/src/lib.rs#L175-L205) returns `<bin_dir>\codex-code-mode-host.exe`. That is a plain drive path: `AbsolutePathBuf` [strips the `\\?\` prefix](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/941b32bf57e520f506cda9741de615f64f53fbe6/codex-rs/utils/absolute-path/src/lib.rs#L147-L150) that `canonicalize` adds.
2. [`ProcessOwnedCodeModeSessionProvider::availability`](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/941b32bf57e520f506cda9741de615f64f53fbe6/codex-rs/code-mode/src/remote_session.rs#L63-L74) passes, because Rust's `fs` APIs convert long paths to `\\?\` form internally.
3. [`Connection::spawn`](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/941b32bf57e520f506cda9741de615f64f53fbe6/codex-rs/code-mode/src/remote_session/connection.rs#L157-L172) calls `Command::new(host_program).spawn()`. Rust's std passes the plain path to `CreateProcessW` as `lpApplicationName`. `codex.exe` has no application manifest at all, so it has no `longPathAware` entry. As a result, `CreateProcessW` rejects any application path of `MAX_PATH` (260) or more UTF-16 units with `ERROR_PATH_NOT_FOUND`, whatever the registry setting.

`CreateProcessW` does accept the same path in `\\?\` form, and that needs no system setting. A `longPathAware` manifest also fixes it, but only on machines where `LongPathsEnabled=1`.

### What steps can reproduce the bug?

You don't need a Codex session or an account for this. The probe below spawns the published `codex-code-mode-host.exe` exactly the way `Connection::spawn` does: piped stdio and `CREATE_NO_WINDOW`. It copies the host into a throwaway folder twice, once at a short path and once at an exact path length. A second copy of the probe has a side-by-side `probe-lpa.exe.manifest` that sets `longPathAware`.

<details><summary>probe source (Rust, no dependencies)</summary>

```rust
use std::os::windows::process::CommandExt;
use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};
use std::process::{Command, Stdio};

#[link(name = "ntdll")]
extern "system" { fn RtlAreLongPathsEnabled() -> u8; }

const NAME: &str = "codex-code-mode-host.exe";

fn try_spawn(label: &str, program: &Path) {
    let mut command = Command::new(program);
    command
        .creation_flags(0x0800_0000) // CREATE_NO_WINDOW, as in Connection::spawn
        .stdin(Stdio::piped())
        .stdout(Stdio::piped())
        .stderr(Stdio::piped());
    let len = program.to_string_lossy().len();
    match command.spawn() {
        Ok(mut child) => {
            std::thread::sleep(std::time::Duration::from_millis(1500));
            let state = match child.try_wait() {
                Ok(None) => "running after 1.5s".to_string(),
                Ok(Some(status)) => format!("exited {status}"),
                Err(e) => format!("try_wait error {e}"),
            };
            let _ = child.kill();
            let _ = child.wait();
            println!("{label:<14} len={len:<4} is_file={} spawn=OK ({state})", program.is_file());
        }
        Err(e) => println!("{label:<14} len={len:<4} is_file={} spawn=ERR {e}", program.is_file()),
    }
}

fn main() {
    // probe <published codex-code-mode-host.exe> <empty temp folder> <target path length>
    let args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();
    let (src, base, target_len) = (PathBuf::from(&args[1]), PathBuf::from(&args[2]), args[3].parse::<usize>().unwrap());
    println!("RtlAreLongPathsEnabled={}", unsafe { RtlAreLongPathsEnabled() });

    let short = base.join("s");
    std::fs::create_dir_all(&short).unwrap();
    std::fs::copy(&src, short.join(NAME)).unwrap();

    let mut deep = base.join("d");
    loop {
        let remaining = target_len as isize - (deep.to_string_lossy().len() + 1 + NAME.len()) as isize;
        if remaining <= 0 { break; }
        deep.push("x".repeat((remaining - 1).clamp(1, 60) as usize));
    }
    std::fs::create_dir_all(&deep).unwrap();
    let long = deep.join(NAME);
    std::fs::copy(&src, &long).unwrap();

    try_spawn("short", &short.join(NAME));
    try_spawn("long", &long);
    try_spawn("long-verbatim", &PathBuf::from(format!(r"\\?\{}", long.display())));
}
```

</details>

The host binary is the published 0.163.0-alpha.11 `codex-code-mode-host.exe` (SHA-256 `9dc96ce0784580f609bfe0e316629df64d9463478f95d6540c7a7746512b285b`). Results on this machine, which has `LongPathsEnabled=1`:

| Parent process | Host path length | Plain path | `\\?\` path |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| no manifest (like `codex.exe`) | 77 | starts | starts |
| no manifest | 259 | starts | starts |
| no manifest | 260 | **os error 3** | starts |
| no manifest | 264 | **os error 3** | starts |
| `longPathAware` manifest | 264 | starts | starts |

"Starts" means the host process was still running 1.5 s after spawn. The probe printed `RtlAreLongPathsEnabled=0` without the manifest and `1` with it.

### What is the expected behavior?

Codex should be able to start its own bundled helpers wherever the package is installed, including paths of 260 characters or more. If it can't, the error should say the path is too long rather than "cannot find".

### Additional information

**Proposed fix.** On Windows, convert long drive paths to `\\?\` form right before spawning. I compiled the helper below and ran it on this machine: the 264-character case starts, and short paths are passed through unchanged. Codex doesn't accept external PRs, so here is the sketch:

```rust
// codex-rs/code-mode/src/remote_session/connection.rs
let mut command = Command::new(spawnable_program(host_program));

/// CreateProcessW rejects an application path of MAX_PATH (260) or more UTF-16
/// units unless the process is long-path aware, but accepts the same path in
/// its `\\?\` form. Only drive-letter paths are rewritten.
#[cfg(windows)]
fn spawnable_program(path: &Path) -> std::borrow::Cow<'_, Path> {
    use std::os::windows::ffi::OsStrExt;
    use std::path::{Component, Prefix};
    const MAX_PATH: usize = 260;
    let is_disk_path = matches!(
        path.components().next(),
        Some(Component::Prefix(prefix)) if matches!(prefix.kind(), Prefix::Disk(_))
    );
    if !is_disk_path || path.as_os_str().encode_wide().count() < MAX_PATH {
        return std::borrow::Cow::Borrowed(path);
    }
    let mut verbatim = std::ffi::OsString::from(r"\\?\");
    verbatim.push(path.as_os_str());
    std::borrow::Cow::Owned(verbatim.into())
}

#[cfg(not(windows))]
fn spawnable_program(path: &Path) -> std::borrow::Cow<'_, Path> {
    std::borrow::Cow::Borrowed(path)
}
```

This is safe for the host because `code_mode_host_program` already returns an absolute, normalized path with backslashes, which a verbatim path requires. The helper could live in a shared crate so that other helpers spawned from the package folder get the same treatment. In this package, `codex-resources\voice\bin\codex-voice-host.exe` is 282 characters at this install path. A test could copy `cargo_bin("codex-code-mode-host")` into a nested temp folder whose path is at least 260 characters, then assert that `Connection::spawn` completes the handshake. Without the fix, that test fails with os error 3 on any Windows runner.

A `longPathAware` manifest for `codex.exe` would also help with other long-path file operations, but it only takes effect when `LongPathsEnabled=1`, so it shouldn't be the only fix.

**Workaround.** Copy the whole `vendor\x86_64-pc-windows-msvc` folder to a short path and run `codex.exe` from there.

**Related:** #48062 and #50268 cover the Windows sandbox setup helper's `CreateFileW` checks on a deep desktop runtime cache. They also involve a missing `longPathAware`, but they are a different code path and product surface from this npm CLI spawn failure.
